Week 5 – Model Evaluation and Reporting Strategy

Overview

To evaluate the regression models developed using the Daily Commodity Prices India data set. This helped me understand what model gave the best results and identify any errors.

Objectives

Evaluate the models using MAE, RMSE and R2 Score Compare different models and how they performed. Produce graphics to show actual and predicted values. Identify any errors and potential issues with the model. Produce an executive summary with recommendations.

Methodology

The models were tested on the testing set. Using the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) to calculate the average error by taking the absolute value of the error. Calculating the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) to see larger errors were weighted a lot. Finally the R2 score tells you how much of the variability in commodity prices can be explained by the model.

We produced some graphics to help us understand the models. We compared the RMSE score. Plotted a scatter graph to show actual vs predicted. Then a histogram to look at the errors.

Results

Decision Tree gave the lowest RMSE.

MAE = 271.08 RMSE = 788.17 R2 Score = 0.690

Random Forest had a lower MAE than Decision Tree but had a higher RMSE. Decision Tree was chosen because of the RMSE but other metrics were considered.

Limitations and Improvements

The model might not consider all the factors that affect the commodity price such as weather, supply, demand, transport costs and government policies. Some predictions had high errors. To improve in the future we could look at using historical price variables, time based validation, analyse different commodities and markets and regularly monitor the model.

